Grammalecte for Collabora Online

Grammalecte is a grammar checker for French. Collabora Online talks to it
over HTTP, the way it talks to LanguageTool and the Duden Korrekturserver,
and underlines what it finds as you type. This directory builds the service
and shows how to run it next to Collabora Online.

Nothing of Grammalecte is in this repository. The Dockerfile fetches the
release archive from grammalecte.net while the image builds, checks its
SHA-256, and runs the server it contains as the user "grammalecte".
Grammalecte is released under the GPL 3; the licence text is in the
archive and in the image.

Build and try it:

    docker build -t grammalecte docker/grammalecte
    docker run --rm -p 8080:8080 grammalecte
    curl -d 'text=Il%20sont%20parti.' http://127.0.0.1:8080/gc_text/fr

The answer lists an agreement error on "sont parti".

Running it next to Collabora Online:

    cd docker/grammalecte
    docker compose up

compose.yaml starts the collabora/code image with the checker enabled and
pointed at the service, on a network the service shares with nothing else.
In coolwsd.xml the same two settings are

    <grammalecte>
        <enabled>true</enabled>
        <base_url>http://grammalecte:8080</base_url>
    </grammalecte>

allowed_languages in coolwsd.xml does not limit the checker: it only
decides which local dictionaries, hyphenation patterns, thesauri and
Lightproof rules are loaded at start, and a remote checker checks every
language it is configured for.

The service has no authentication and no TLS. Anyone who can reach it can
send it text, so keep it on an internal network, as compose.yaml does, and
never on a public address.

What the checker looks for is chosen per user in the Options dialog, under
the Grammalecte section: each rule family of the checker is a switch there.
The server settings themselves show in that dialog only where the
administrator allows it, with allow_user_settings in coolwsd.xml.

Collabora Office, the desktop application, has the same checker and is
configured in its own Options dialog. Packaging Grammalecte with the
desktop application is a later step.
